737 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097737 #edb/ref 1061 430121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097737 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "961ff0d0-9cef-5a2d-8207-63481c0cc1ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097737 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097738 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097738 #edb/ref 1061 430122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097738 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e301e3f9-501f-5f63-afac-f53379b62c13" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097738 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097739 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097739 #edb/ref 1061 430125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097739 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "957d3735-2041-5bec-9e7c-4c1623dd4899" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097739 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097740 #edb/ref 1061 430128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097740 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "11ad8934-d3f9-546c-b161-9058d251ac66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097740 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097833 #edb/ref 1064 "A **unit vector** is a vector whose magnitude is equal to $1$.\n\nGiven any vector $\\mathbf a$, we can create a unit vector $\\mathbf u$ by dividing $\\mathbf a$ by its magnitude $\\mid \\, \\mathbf{a} \\, \\mid$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid}\n$$\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\mathbf{a}\n$$\n\n![](images/c7/c78d5b75c1d5928e7b269a4a67229487b3d3324e9815b11c5fd1f47f63437d82.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097833 #edb/ref 1065 2906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097833 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a90fd4b3-d41f-589f-9067-39d57e3e8712" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097833 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100823 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100823 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100823 #edb/ref 1039 "Applying the Product Rule to Compute a Limit Given a Graph" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100823 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100823 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "23567a86-8e1e-5b94-bac6-09c79cdb42c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100824 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100824 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100824 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100824 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100824 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186235427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100824 #edb/ref 1039 "Applying the Product Rule to Compute a Limit" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100824 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100824 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "5feb914d-efad-5d89-85a9-db99befc9928" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100952 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100952 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100952 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186235468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100952 #edb/ref 1039 "Applying the Quotient Rule to Compute a Limit" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100952 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100952 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "0a649e49-73c7-5470-8361-2002da74f234" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100953 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100953 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100953 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186235507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100953 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100953 #edb/ref 1039 "Applying the Quotient Rule to Compute a Limit Given a Graph" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100953 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100953 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b0159149-010c-5bef-a28d-85c4c43f0586" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101087 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101602 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101087 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101087 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101087 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101087 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101087 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101087 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101087 #edb/ref 1045 "The Product and Quotient Rules for Limits" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101087 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186101796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101087 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101087 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "af8776aa-b3ed-5305-95b5-bf42623f4ed3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101602 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101602 #edb/ref 1061 12001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101602 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2408ea61-ca9b-52c5-ae47-17af57a34e64" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101602 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101603 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101602 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101603 #edb/ref 1061 12002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101603 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a3d559f2-c362-56ee-804c-080f88071508" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101603 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101796 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101796 #edb/ref 1061 333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101796 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "55b6421a-40da-531f-9d53-41329827a526" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101796 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101797 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101797 #edb/ref 1061 339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101797 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "67532783-b8e9-5de2-9ca2-19a49fbf6ec4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101797 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101798 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101798 #edb/ref 1061 340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101798 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e8f2b1af-f0df-5d06-b349-280fb90373de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101798 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101799 #edb/ref 1061 342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101799 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1da33225-c9cd-5fd9-8576-b2d8fdce7dcf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101799 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101928 #edb/ref 1064 "The **product rule** states that the limit of the product of two functions equals the product of the limits.\n\nMore precisely, if\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\, a}f(x)=L\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\, a}g(x)=K\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → a)(f(x) \\cdot g(x)) &= lim_(x → a)f(x) \\cdot lim_(x → a)g(x) \\\\\n&= L \\cdot K\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, we can evaluate\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\,2} \\left[ (x^2 +2x +3)(x^3-2x-1) \\right]\n$$\n\nby evaluating the limit of each polynomial separately, and then multiplying the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2)[(x^{2} + 2x + 3)(x^{3} - 2x - 1)] &= lim_(x → 2)(x^{2} + 2x + 3) \\cdot lim_(x → 2)(x^{3} - 2x - 1) \\\\\n&= (2^{2} + 2 \\cdot 2 + 3) \\cdot (2^{3} - 2 \\cdot 2 - 1) \\\\\n&= (4 + 4 + 3) \\cdot (8 - 4 - 1) \\\\\n&= (11) \\cdot (3) \\\\\n&= 33\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101928 #edb/ref 1065 175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101928 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c7f68019-2904-5740-87e6-e66d189cb771" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101928 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101929 #edb/ref 1064 "The **quotient rule** states that the limit of the quotient of two functions equals the quotient of the limits (provided that the limit of the denominator is not zero).\n\nMore precisely, if\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}g(x)= K \\neq 0\n$$\n\n, then\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a} \\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)} = \\dfrac{\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)}{\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}g(x)}=\\dfrac{L}{K}\n$$\n\nFor example, we can evaluate\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\,2} \\dfrac{x^2+4x}{x+1}\n$$\n\nby evaluating the limits of the numerator and denominator separately, and then dividing the results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2)\\frac{x^{2} + 4x}{x + 1} &= (lim_(x → 2)(x^{2} + 4x))/(lim_(x → 2)(x + 1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2^{2} + 4 \\cdot 2}{2 + 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12}{3} \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101929 #edb/ref 1065 178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101929 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "46d526ad-25bf-58f5-88e0-695b4ff6d430" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101929 #edb/ref 1067 "The Quotient Rule for Limits" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105903 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105903 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105903 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105903 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105903 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186295109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105903 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186295110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105903 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186295123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] 