 [17592186105905 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105905 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105905 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Area of a Quadrilateral" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105905 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105905 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3173d361-b91d-5699-97d8-6595ef59d0a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105906 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105906 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105906 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105906 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105906 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105906 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105906 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105906 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105906 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Area of a Triangle Given the Area of a Quadrilateral" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105906 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105906 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "5dd3dcff-06d2-5d61-9863-0f5b541d8d4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105954 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105954 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105954 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105954 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105954 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105954 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105954 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105954 #edb/ref 1045 "The Area of a General Triangle" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105954 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186106917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105954 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105954 #edb/ref 1050 720.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186105954 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "75a4e4a8-271a-52f6-80d3-a6bbb0833cfd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106917 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106917 #edb/ref 1061 463833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106917 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e26490ba-2418-5a78-9c31-5425d1b67c42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106917 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106918 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106918 #edb/ref 1061 463834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106918 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ee7e485d-cba2-595c-b5bd-d8cd98cc7e04" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106918 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106919 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106919 #edb/ref 1061 463837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106919 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ca664e6a-cebd-5079-927f-7fc062b9c6fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106919 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106920 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106920 #edb/ref 1061 463840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106920 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ab2a0c05-ebeb-54b0-a9a3-b37d1f143e15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106920 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106921 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106921 #edb/ref 1061 463843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106921 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "85b3474e-aa3e-5a9b-8c47-069d6e314fad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106921 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106922 #edb/ref 1061 463846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106922 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "29c5a503-b01d-5845-a29d-0bd76807b33f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106922 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107027 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider $\\triangle ABC$ with sides of lengths $a,b,$ and $c,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1d30e12e2252b27a651bb02b718cf2214c7ddcf4f0e768c5bd952c11b2e4ecde.png)\n\nLet's now add the height of length $h$ perpendicular to $\\overline{BC},$ as shown.\n\n![](images/96/96817ab2168b9ec37777166a26001ee8bc7e7709f80fdf234ac6d70cdfbb5ddc.png)\n\nUsing the usual formula for the area $\\mathcal A$ of a triangle in terms of its base and height, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A = \\dfrac12 ah.\n$$\n\nUsing elementary trigonometry, we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{h}{b} = \\sin C\n$$\n\nwhich means that \n\n$$\nh = b\\sin C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by substituting this into our expression for $\\mathcal A,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A = \\dfrac12ab\\sin C\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nWe can use a similar approach to prove the following formulas:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}A \\\\ A & =\\frac{1}{2}ac\\operatorname{sin}B\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107027 #edb/ref 1065 13277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107027 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e126d83f-0c01-514b-bb7e-ce2d16291b22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107027 #edb/ref 1067 "Deriving the Formula for the Area of a General Triangle" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107063 #edb/ref 1064 "The formula for the area of a general triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\frac{1}{2}{\\color{red}{a}}{\\color{blue}{b}}\\sin{C},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\color{red}a$ and $\\color{blue}b$ are two sides of the triangle and $C$ is the measure of the angle between those sides (called the **included angle**).\n\nWe'll prove this result at the end of the lesson.\n\nTo demonstrate how this works, suppose that we want to calculate the area of the non-right-angled triangle below:\n\n![](images/4b/4b0fb75f9b2eaf3d0237d8cfe2e31bc27b02425b6cffa8d15f27d6de03f60838.png)\n\nLet's call the lengths of the sides $BC = \\color{red}{a}$ and $AC ={\\color{blue}{b}}.$ For the triangle above we have\n${\\color{red}a=10},$ ${\\color{blue}b=12},$ and  $m\\angle C=45^{\\circ}.$\n\nTherefore, the area of our triangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10\\cdot 12\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{45}^{∘} \\\\  & =60\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =30\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107063 #edb/ref 1065 2465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107063 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ff9f9791-f688-59c5-8c9d-b776016045be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107063 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115259 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115259 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115259 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115259 #edb/ref 1039 "Plotting a Horizontal Translation of a Given Graph" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115259 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186115088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115259 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "372e7248-7fe0-5138-896d-61b4866f83cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115260 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115260 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115260 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Corresponding Point on a Horizontally Translated Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115260 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186115089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115260 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "919da198-25e0-5b6f-8d99-79f1508759b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115261 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115261 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115261 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115261 #edb/ref 1039 "Plotting Horizontal and Vertical Translations of a Given Graph" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115261 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186115090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115261 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d7c84dd5-2c76-5584-81a1-53d2459afb94" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115274 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115274 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115274 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Corresponding Point on a Horizontally and Vertically Translated Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115274 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186115103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115274 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "57ef1e2c-07d4-5d04-9de8-8884cae17c00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115418 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115418 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115418 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115418 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115418 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115418 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115418 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115418 #edb/ref 1045 "Horizontal Translations of Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115418 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186116148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115418 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115418 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "abceb987-e559-5c80-8a21-441171253b50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116148 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116148 #edb/ref 1061 18017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116148 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6345469f-1236-5eaf-8f5f-c51d47bf4f3c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116148 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116149 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116149 #edb/ref 1061 18018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116149 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d171b522-79a1-5dbc-bcf1-aa7179ecd080" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116149 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116150 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116150 #edb/ref 1061 18019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116150 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b5c16920-e146-5ef0-841d-e2645a4e1de6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116150 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116151 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116151 #edb/ref 1061 18020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116151 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c6cc0af1-c408-599c-ac0a-69b7af985fac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116151 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116152 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116152 #edb/ref 1061 18022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116152 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b18fd01e-e160-5b17-a9cf-730b2b261357" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116152 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116174 #edb/ref 1061 19261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116174 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5cdd1f56-3cc3-5455-896a-aec5448b7269" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116174 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116503 #edb/ref 1064 "In general, for $k>0$,\n\n- the graph of $y=f(x-k)$ is a horizontal translation of $y=f(x)$ by $k$ units to the *right*, while\n- the graph of $y=f(x+k)$ is a horizontal translation of $y=f(x)$ by $k$ units to the *left*.\n\n**Watch out!** It may seem counterintuitive that subtracting $k$ moves the graph to the right, while adding $k$ moves the graph to the left. However, this is in fact true.\n\nTo see why, let's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ shown below. Let's use this graph to plot the graph of $y=f(x-2)$.\n\n![](images/6c/6ca96bba855899f9b87ae06c166503c9af739fcd6bb2ec466a73b75cc70dde7f.png)\n\nLet's take some points on the graph of $y=f(x)$ and find the corresponding points on the graphs of $y=f(x-2)$.\n\nFor example, take the point $(3,-4)$. The fact that it's on the graph of $y=f(x)$ means that\n\n$$\nf(3)=-4\n$$\n\nThen if\n\n$$\nx={\\color{red}5}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\nf(x-2) = f({\\color{red}5}-2) = f(3) = -4\n$$\n\nwhich means that $({\\color{red}5},-4)$ is on the graph of $y=f(x-2)$.\n\nFor each point on the graph of $y=f(x)$ there is a corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x-2)$ with the same $y$-value and a $x$-value that is $2$ units *greater*! Several more examples of points are shown below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Points on [math]y=f(x)[/math] & Points on [math]y=f(x-2)[/math] \\\\ (3,-4) & (5,-4) \\\\ (1, 2) & (3, 2) \\\\ (0, 1.5) & (2, 1.5) \\\\ (-2, 0) & (0, 0)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's plot both curves.\n\n![](images/ed/edae088984432dc7d0fff816a9e016ad221a3c897a78cf79efe6c784fa9176fe.png)\n\nWe see that every point on the graph of $y=f(x-2)$ is shifted $2$ units *to the right* of the corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116503 #edb/ref 1065 3280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116503 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "da231b95-be14-58c3-a8fe-6261d61c7d83" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116503 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116504 #edb/ref 1064 "When graphing a function, we can combine vertical and horizontal translations. In general, the graph of\n\n$$\ny = f(x-h)+k\n$$\n\nis a translation of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $h$ units to the right and $k$ units up.\n\nIt doesn't matter which translation we do first, but it's often easiest to start with the horizontal translation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116504 #edb/ref 1065 3282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116504 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ceb4fcff-ae84-5217-aa0c-16d64fc23eac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116504 #edb/ref 1067 "Combining Vertical and Horizontal Translations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119751 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119751 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119751 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120489 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119751 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119751 #edb/ref 1039 "Representing Angles in the Coordinate Plane With the Unit Circle" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119751 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119751 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "24d0f296-256c-5da4-8992-e2112dfe99b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119752 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119752 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119752 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119752 #edb/ref 1039 "Representing Angles in Radians in the Coordinate Plane With the Unit Circle" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119752 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119752 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6435eb94-76f8-5e46-b005-03451ab6eba2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119753 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119753 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119753 #edb/ref 1039 "Using Trigonometry to Find an Angle in the Coordinate Plane" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119753 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119753 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "50de296d-56a1-5166-aa7d-42de027a33bc" #edb/ref 175921860